  id: "pa105", domain: "para-nematodes",
  q: "Discuss the ectopic and gynaecological manifestations of Enterobius vermicularis.",
  tags: ["Enterobius", "vulvovaginitis", "appendicitis", "ectopic"],
  keypoints: ["Enterobius vermicularis", "perianal skin", "autoinfection", "vulvovaginitis", "salpingitis", "pelvic peritonitis", "appendicitis", "eosinophilic granuloma", "cellophane tape test", "albendazole", "pyrantel pamoate", "household contacts"],
  answer: `
    <h4>Life cycle relevant to ectopic disease</h4>
    <p><strong>Enterobius vermicularis</strong> adults live in the caecum and appendix. Gravid females migrate at night to the <strong>perianal skin</strong> to lay eggs, causing pruritus ani. Scratching transfers eggs to fingers and fomites, and eggs become infective within hours, so <strong>autoinfection</strong> and household spread are common. Eosinophilia is usually absent because the worm does not invade tissue.</p>
    <h4>Gynaecological spread</h4>
    <p>In girls and women, larvae can enter the vulva and vagina, causing <strong>vulvovaginitis</strong>, pruritus vulvae, discharge and dysuria. Ascending migration may reach the uterus, fallopian tubes and peritoneum, producing <strong>salpingitis</strong>, oophoritis or <strong>pelvic peritonitis</strong>. Peritoneal <strong>eosinophilic granuloma</strong> containing eggs or worm fragments can mimic endometriosis or malignancy at laparoscopy.</p>
    <h4>Appendicitis and other sites</h4>
    <p>Worms obstruct the appendiceal lumen and provoke inflammation, so <em>Enterobius</em> is found in a proportion of appendicectomy specimens; whether it is the cause or an incidental finding is debated. Rare ectopic sites include the urinary tract, liver and lung. Granuloma around degenerating worms is the histological clue.</p>
    <h4>Diagnosis and treatment</h4>
    <p>Diagnose with the <strong>cellophane tape test</strong> — three consecutive mornings before bathing — or by finding adult worms. Eggs may be seen in vaginal secretions or urine. Treat with <strong>albendazole</strong>, mebendazole or <strong>pyrantel pamoate</strong>, repeated after two weeks, and treat all <strong>household contacts</strong>. Nail trimming, hand washing and hot laundering of bedding interrupt reinfection.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Persistent vulvar itching or discharge in a child.</li>
      <li>Recurrent urinary symptoms without bacterial growth.</li>
      <li>Right lower quadrant pain suggesting appendiceal involvement.</li>
      <li>A peritoneal granuloma found incidentally at surgery.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>These presentations should prompt a tape test even when stool microscopy is negative.</p>
  `,
  tip: "Most candidates forget the gynaecological route — lead with vulvovaginitis and salpingitis.",
  sources: ["P1", "P2", "P4"]
